  "question": {
    "text": "A monoatomic ideal gas, initially at temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is enclosed in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless piston. The gas is allowed to expand adiabatically to a temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> by releasing the piston suddenly. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;are the lengths of the gas column, before and after the expansion respectively, then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math> will be:"
  },
